<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Karashahr</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Yanqi, Kara-Shahr, Agni, Karasahr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Karashahr, called Yanqi in Chinese sources, is an oasis town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarim-basin">Tarim Basin</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>, north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. In antiquity it was the centre of a Tocharian-speaking Buddhist city state on the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> and Kucha. The state paid tribute at times to Chinese dynasties and to steppe powers such as the Rouran and the Turks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/karashahr.jpg" alt="Karashahr" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Rolf Müller · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mosque_yanqi_xinjiang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Karashahr, called Yanqi in Chinese sources, lies on the Kaidu River about 24 kilometres west of Lake Bosten in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xinjiang">Xinjiang</a>. The lake, about 1,000 square kilometres, has been noted since Han times for its fish. In classical times the city stood on the branch of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk-road">Silk Road</a> that ran along the northern edge of the Taklamakan Desert. It was originally known in the Tocharian languages as Ārśi, and later as Agni, perhaps from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> word for fire. Its neighbours were the related states of Kucha to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turfan">Turfan</a> to the east and Krorän to the south.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Han sources describe Yanqi as a large and important kingdom, controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> extended its influence in the late 2nd century BCE. From the 1st century BCE the Ārśi converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. After Yanqi attacked and killed the Chinese Protector General Chen Mu in 75 CE, General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-chao">Ban Chao</a> made a punitive campaign in 94 CE, and the king was beheaded. A source describes it as having 15,000 households and 52,000 individuals. Agnideśa became a tributary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tang-dynasty">Tang China</a> in 632, and in 644 Emperor Taizong attacked it after it allied with the Turks. One of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-garrisons-of-anxi">Four Garrisons of Anxi</a> was based there.</p><p>By the mid-9th century the Uyghur Khaganate had conquered the area, the Tocharian languages were fading, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> gained ground and the town became known by the Turkic name Karasahr, &quot;black city&quot;. From the mid-13th to the 18th century it was part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chagatai-khanate">Chagatai Khanate</a>. The Jesuit Bento de Góis stayed three months in 1605 in the kingdom he called Cialis, and the British traveller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francis-younghusband">Francis Younghusband</a> visited in 1887.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Yanqi is the seat of Yanqi Hui Autonomous County in the Bayingolin Mongol Autonomous Prefecture. It lies on China National Highway 314 and the Southern Xinjiang Railway, and had 31,773 people in 2006, of whom 16,032 were Han, 7,781 Hui and 7,154 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uyghurs">Uyghur</a>. It has many mosques.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Karasahr” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The largest were Kucha, with 81,000 inhabitants, and <b>Agni</b>, with 32,000.</blockquote><a href="/culture/tocharians">Tocharians · Society</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Karasahr</b>, called Yanqi in Chinese and Agni in its own documents, grew around the Bosten Lake oasis on the north-eastern edge of the Tarim Basin.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-karasahr">Kingdom of Karasahr</a></li><li><blockquote>The Turpan khans ruled the oases of Turpan, <b>Kara-Shahr</b> and at times Hami, controlling trade routes into China.</blockquote><a href="/state/turpan-khanate">Turpan Khanate</a></li><li><blockquote>The Shatapatha Brahmana tells how Videgha Mathava, guided by the fire god <b>Agni</b>, carried Vedic culture east across the Sadanira river, probably the Gandak, into the marshy lands of Mithila.</blockquote><a href="/state/videha">Videha</a></li></ul></section>
